  "referenceSolution": "def pacific_atlantic(heights: list[list[int]]) -> list[list[int]]:\n    rows, cols = len(heights), len(heights[0])\n\n    def reach(starts):\n        seen = set(starts)\n        stack = list(starts)\n        while stack:\n            r, c = stack.pop()\n            for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n                if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols and (nr, nc) not in seen and heights[nr][nc] >= heights[r][c]:\n                    seen.add((nr, nc))\n                    stack.append((nr, nc))\n        return seen\n\n    pacific = reach([(0, c) for c in range(cols)] + [(r, 0) for r in range(1, rows)])\n    atlantic = reach([(rows - 1, c) for c in range(cols)] + [(r, cols - 1) for r in range(rows - 1)])\n    return [[r, c] for r, c in sorted(pacific & atlantic)]\n",
  "targetComplexity": {"time": "O(rows * cols)", "space": "O(rows * cols)"},
  "hints": [
    "Pick one cell and ask whether it reaches the Pacific. What would you do for every cell, and what does that cost across the whole grid?",
    "Instead of asking where each cell's water goes, ask which cells can reach a given ocean. Where would you start searching from?",
    "Water flows downhill. If you start at the ocean's border and search inland, in which direction may each step move?",
    "Think about equal heights: does water cross between two neighbouring cells of the same height? Try a grid where every cell has height 2.",
    "Run one search inward from all Pacific border cells and one from all Atlantic border cells, only stepping to neighbours that are at least as high, then intersect the two reachable sets."
  ],
  "followUps": [
    "What are the time and space complexity, and why is this better than searching from every cell?",
    "Why do you search uphill from the oceans instead of downhill from each cell?",
    "How does your code treat neighbouring cells of equal height, and what would change if water could only flow strictly downhill?",
    "If you used recursive DFS, what could go wrong on a large grid and how would you avoid it?",
    "How would you extend the solution to a third ocean touching the corners, or to diagonal flow?"
  ],
  "idealAnswerPoints": [
    "Rejects the per-cell search (roughly O((rows * cols)^2)) and reverses the direction of the search",
    "Starts a multi-source DFS or BFS from every Pacific border cell and another from every Atlantic border cell",
    "Moves only to neighbours with height >= the current cell, which is the reverse of water flow",
    "Intersects the two reachable sets to get the answer",
    "States O(rows * cols) time and space and treats equal heights as connected"
  ],
  "weakAnswerSigns": [
    "Searches downhill from every cell separately, which is far too slow on a 70 x 70 grid",
    "Steps to strictly higher cells only, so plateaus are wrongly blocked",
    "Seeds only one corner or one edge of each ocean instead of the whole border",
    "Returns cells as (col, row) instead of [row, col]"
  ],
  "rubric": {
    "correctness": "Exactly the cells that reach both oceans on every test, including single cells, rows and columns, flat plateaus and bowl shapes.",
    "efficiency": "Two multi-source searches, O(rows * cols). Searching from every cell separately fails the large grids.",
    "code_quality": "One reusable search helper called for both oceans, tidy bounds checks and a clear visited structure.",
    "communication": "Explains why the search runs from the oceans uphill and walks through the small example."
  }
